{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-opus-5-5","id":"msg_011Ch1GwNsDPaWofSC9CU7GL","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"tool_use","id":"toolu_01ALntv4fGANazRzhP6MRN5Z","name":"StructuredOutput","input":{"report":"PAYLOAD 8/32 — what it covers: new files under .claude/hooks/_lib/_context_budget_parts/ (middle_out, status, summarization), _cost_transcripts_parts/ (__init__, cli, pricing, records, rollup, routing, routing_helpers) and _installer_write_safety/ (__init__, commands, engine, model, operands). These are the PLAN-197 extractions behind the thin entry points .claude/scripts/context-budget.py, ceo-cost-transcripts.py and check-installer-write-safety.py. I read the three facades and the sibling components that the facades bind together (compaction, inventory, syntax, verdicts_b, report) so I could follow every global name and class identity. Limitation: no interpreter was available in this review (file tools only), so every finding below comes from reading the code. None of it was executed.\n\nDECLARED CONDITIONS AGAINST THIS PAYLOAD\n- Condition 7 (thin entry points, logic moved into _lib modules) holds for all three tools. Each entry point keeps its path and binds the extracted declarations into its own globals. Condition 7 promises no behavioral identity, so the finding below does not make it false.\n- Condition 5: none of these three packages is in the 13 new kernel lines, and nothing in this payload claims otherwise.\n- Conditions 6, 8 and 4: this payload does not touch them.\n- Shipping: install.sh copies each .claude/hooks/_lib/* entry, subdirectories included, unless _framework_path_excluded matches it, and none of the new package names match. Adopters therefore get the modules the facades import.\n- Python: stdlib only, and every file uses `from __future__ import annotations`. I found no syntax newer than 3.9.\n- Name resolution: every global the moved functions use resolves in the facade namespace. Checked: _clamp_pct, _env_int, estimate_tokens, _read_text, _clamp_nonneg_int, CAT_MCP, THRESHOLD_SKILL_LINES, _SCRIPT_DIR, _rp, _parse_ts, Tok, canon_operand, _KEYWORDS and alias_set.\n- Hooks: none of these modules is a hook, and no hook imports them, so the fail-open/fail-closed rule for hooks does not apply here.\n- Writes: all three tools are read-only. The routing reader keeps its symlink and corpus-confinement checks (_inside).\n\nNEW FINDINGS (annex)\n\nP1 — .claude/hooks/_lib/_installer_write_safety/__init__.py:36 (_bind_class), combined with syntax.py:10 (`class Incomplete(ParseError)`).\nDefect: _bind_class clones every component class with `type(original)(name, original.__bases__, attrs)`. ParseError is cloned to ParseError' with bases (Exception,). Incomplete is cloned to Incomplete' with bases (the ORIGINAL ParseError,), not the clone. On the facade, `issubclass(Incomplete, ParseError)` is therefore False. Every bound function looks up `raise Incomplete` as Incomplete' and `except ParseError` as ParseError', so the `except ParseError` sites no longer catch Incomplete. Affected sites: commands.py:131, operands.py:35, model.py:320, 344 and 632, and verdicts_b.py:141, 440 and 447. In the old single-file module they did catch it. build_logical_lines is not affected because it catches Incomplete by name.\nConcrete scenario: any scanned scripts/**/*.sh with a fully quoted dd operand, e.g. `dd if=/dev/zero \"of=$dst\" bs=1 count=0`. In classify_command (commands.py:516-518), canon_operand(\"\\\"of=$dst\\\"\") starts with of=, so the destination becomes Tok(\"$dst\\\"\"). _record_command then calls _record_occ, which calls canon_operand, which calls _read_dquote. That raises Incomplete' (\"unterminated double quote\"), operands.py:35 does not catch it, and nothing up to main() catches it either. Result: a traceback and rc 1, where the base absorbed the error and recorded the site. The same thing happens for an unbalanced `${` in a sed side read with honour_single_quotes=False (commands.py:131), and for a lone quote that lex sees after a `#` comment inside a `$( )` body (model.py:344, verdicts_b.py:447).\nImpact: this is a maintainer tool. The validate.yml step goes red, so the failure stays closed, not open, and no adopter session is affected. But a census designed to turn unparseable input into a blocking site now crashes instead, and the facade's promise of a \"complete module namespace\" for downstream harnesses is broken: catching ParseError around lex() misses Incomplete. The current corpus does not trigger it (there is no `dd ... of=` in scripts/), so the defect is latent.\nMinimal fix: in _bind_class, return exception classes uncloned (`if issubclass(original, BaseException): return original`), or keep an original-to-clone map and swap cloned bases into `__bases__`. Add two regression tests: assert issubclass(mod.Incomplete, mod.ParseError), and run the census on a fixture containing `dd \"of=$dst\"`.\n\nP2 FOLLOW-UPS\n1. .claude/hooks/_lib/_cost_transcripts_parts/__init__.py:55 — the class re-binding loop rebinds every FunctionType whose `__module__` equals the component's. For a dataclass, that includes the recursive-repr wrapper on `__repr__`. The wrapper is created by dataclasses/reprlib, gets the component's `__module__` through wraps (or an explicit copy), and needs the global `_thread` (3.9-3.11) or `get_ident` (3.12+). Neither exists in the ceo-cost-transcripts.py namespace. Result: repr() and str() of UsageRecord, ScanCounters, PricingResult and Priced raise NameError. I found no production caller that formats these objects, so the damage is limited to debugging and test diagnostics. Fix: rebind only when `method.__code__.co_filename` is the component's own file, or skip dataclass-generated members.\n2. Honesty, stale location comments in the moved text: middle_out.py:22, summarization.py:21 and compaction.py:20 say the code \"lives here in `scripts/`\"; status.py:24 refers to the probes \"above\"; pricing.py:171 and routing_helpers.py:127 point to \"the module docstring\", which now sits in the facade. Fix: update the pointers.\n3. verdicts_b.py:698 (bound by _installer_write_safety/__init__.py:45-48): its `__all__` does not exclude `_commands` / `_commands_name`, so a module object and a string leak into the facade namespace, and commands' classes get cloned a second time. Harmless today, but it widens the re-exported surface. Fix: add both names to the exclusion tuple.\n\nClean in this payload: the context-budget extraction (middle_out, summarization, status), the cost-transcripts window, dedup and routing logic, and the installer-census engine, commands and model logic match the facades' documented contracts. I found nothing that disables a governance hook, changes model or effort, or blocks a legitimate session.","decision":"GO-WITH-CONDITIONS","sentence":"No declared condition is false against this payload and I found no P0; the one undeclared P1 is latent and fails closed in a maintainer-only tool (on the facade, Incomplete no longer subclasses ParseError after cloning), and it has to be carried as a signed annex finding or fixed."},"caller":{"type":"direct"}}],"container":null,"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":2,"cache_creation_input_tokens":796,"cache_read_input_tokens":192375,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":796},"output_tokens":24,"service_tier":"standard","inference_geo":"global","speed":"standard"},"input_transformations":[],"diagnostics":null,"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"7affb10d-4e4f-46c9-8efb-a709552287db","uuid":"fbabac4f-4b6e-4674-98d2-6d7db29bc6fb","timestamp":"2026-11-13T16:53:47.386Z","request_id":"req_011Ch1GwNe4ZFeCLMpcMTTtD","wire_tool_inputs":{"toolu_01ALntv4fGANazRzhP6MRN5Z":{"report":"PAYLOAD 8/32 — what it covers: new files under .claude/hooks/_lib/_context_budget_parts/ (middle_out, status, summarization), _cost_transcripts_parts/ (